     <p>Get AI Checked. It is the same phrase behind our original address, <span class="muted">getaicheck.com</span>, which now redirects here to <span class="muted">gaicheck.com</span>. The name describes what you do with it: before you trust, pay, repost, or reply, you get the thing checked.</p>
 
     <h2 id="limits">How accurate is GAIC?</h2>
-    <p><b>There is no single accuracy percentage we can honestly promise.</b> The text tool is a hand-built, English-focused pattern heuristic and has not been validated as an authorship detector. Its 0–100 pattern signal is not a probability.</p>
-    <p>The bundled GAIC Image Model v2 is derived from the <a href="https://huggingface.co/OwensLab/commfor-model-224" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Community Forensics 224 model</a>, trained on content from thousands of generators available through 2024. It can still miss newer generators, heavily compressed or edited media, screenshots, small images, and unfamiliar content. GAIC therefore uses only a conservative high-signal warning band and never labels a low score “authentic.”</p>
+    <p><b>Every check ends with a lean and a confidence level, and none of them is proof.</b> The text tool is an English-focused model trained on public research collections. On collections it never trained on, about 3–5% of human-written documents leaned AI, and high-confidence AI leans were right about 98% of the time. It has not been validated as an authorship detector and can be unfair to non-native, translated, academic, or heavily edited writing.</p>
+    <p>The bundled GAIC Image Model v3 is derived from the <a href="https://huggingface.co/OwensLab/commfor-model-224" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Community Forensics 224 model</a>, trained on content from thousands of generators available through 2024, and is read by a GAIC decision head trained on 2025–2026 generators and on real photos, artwork, charts, and screenshots. Tested on generator families and image sources it never saw in training, it leaned AI on about two-thirds of AI images and screenshots of them, while no kind of real image leaned AI more than 10% of the time (about 4% on average), and high-confidence leans were right about 97–98% of the time. It can still miss new generators and be fooled by heavy edits, compression, or unusual content; a low-confidence lean is a close call. Video is judged from sampled frames and is less certain than a still image.</p>
     <p>These limits are not unique to GAIC. OpenAI <a href="https://openai.com/index/new-ai-classifier-for-indicating-ai-written-text/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">withdrew its own text classifier</a> for low accuracy and warned against primary-decision use. Research has also documented <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11156" target="_blank" rel="noopener">detector evasion and fundamental limits</a> and <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02819" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bias against non-native English writing</a>. That is why GAIC asks you to check drafts, source history, and context instead of accusing someone from a score.</p>
 
